1. PASS encoding= TO EVERY open(), EVEN WHEN IT IS THE DEFAULT
   locale.getpreferredencoding(False) is the default on your machine,
   and it is NOT necessarily utf-8: it is cp1252 on a western Windows
   box and the locale's charset elsewhere. This machine's answer is
   deliberately not printed -- it would make this key a fact about the
   runner, which is the whole problem the habit prevents.
   forbidden:  open('data.csv')
   correct:    open('data.csv', encoding='utf-8')
   Same file, same code, different result on a colleague's laptop -- and
   nothing in the traceback points at the open() call.

2. RUN THE TESTS ONCE UNDER -X warn_default_encoding
   python3 -X warn_default_encoding -W error::EncodingWarning -m pytest
   Every open() that relied on the default becomes a warning naming the
   file and line. It is a one-off audit, not a permanent setting, and it
   is the only way to find the ones you did not write.

3. FILENAMES ARE BYTES WEARING A COSTUME
   sys.getfilesystemencoding()      'utf-8'
   sys.getfilesystemencodeerrors()  'surrogateescape'
   os.fsdecode(b'caf\xe9.txt') -> 'caf\udce9.txt'
   os.fsencode(that)    -> b'caf\xe9.txt'   round trip: True
   The name is not valid UTF-8 and Python still round-trips it, because
   surrogateescape puts each undecodable byte in a lone surrogate. The
   cost: that str cannot be encoded by anything else, so printing it or
   putting it in JSON raises -- correctly.

4. bytes METHODS THAT LOOK LIKE str METHODS ARE ASCII-ONLY
   b'CAF\xc3\x89'.lower() -> b'caf\xc3\x89'
   'CAFÉ'.lower() -> 'café'
   The bytes version lowercased C, A, F and left the two bytes of É
   alone -- because bytes.lower() is defined on ASCII and nothing else.
   That is documented and it is on purpose: bytes have no encoding, so
   there is no other case mapping it could apply. It is still a silent
   wrong answer if you thought you were working with text.

THE ONE-LINE VERSION
   Say the encoding out loud at every boundary, treat filenames as a
   special case that Python has already solved for you, and never let a
   bytes object anywhere near a method whose name suggests it knows
   about language.
